I can provide a complete and well-documented example of how to test public class methods in Java.

Here's an example of a simple calculator class with public methods for addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division:

```java
import static org.junit.Assert.*;

public class Calculator {

    public int add(int num1, int num2) {
        return num1 + num2;
    }

    public int subtract(int num1, int num2) {
        if (num2 == 0) {
            throw new ArithmeticException("Subtraction by zero is not allowed");
        }
        return num1 - num2;
    }

    public int multiply(int num1, int num2) {
        return num1 * num2;
    }

    public double divide(int num1, int num2) {
        if (num2 == 0) {
            throw new ArithmeticException("Division by zero is not allowed");
        }
        return (double) num1 / num2;
    }
}
```

To test these methods, you can create a class that uses the Calculator class and calls its public methods. Here's an example of how to write unit tests for this class:

```java
import org.junit.Test;

public class CalculatorTest {

    @Test
    public void testAdd() {
        Calculator calculator = new Calculator();
        int result = calculator.add(10, 5);
        assertEquals(15, result); // Expected result is 15
    }

    @Test(expected = ArithmeticException.class)
    public void testSubtractZero() {
        Calculator calculator = new Calculator();
        calculator.subtract(10, 0);
    }

    @Test
    public void testMultiply() {
        Calculator calculator = new Calculator();
        int result = calculator.multiply(4, 6);
        assertEquals(24, result); // Expected result is 24
    }

    @Test
    public void testDivide() {
        Calculator calculator = new Calculator();
        double result = calculator.divide(10, 2);
        assertEquals(5.0, result, 0.01); // Expected result is 5.0 with a small tolerance for floating-point precision issues
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TestHelper.testPublicClassMethod();
        TestHelper.testPublicMethodWithParams();
        TestHelper.testPublicStaticMethod();
        new CalculatorTest().runTests();
    }

    private void runTests() {
        System.out.println("Running tests...");
        try {
            for (CalculatorTest.Test test : CalculatorTest.class.getMethods()) {
                if (test != CalculatorTest.$Test.class) {
                    test.runTest();
                    System.out.println(test.getName() + " passed");
                } else {
                    System.out.println(test.getName() + " is a method");
                }
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("Error running tests: " + e.getMessage());
        }

        System.out.println("\nTests completed.");
    }
}
```

In this example, we have created a CalculatorTest class that contains methods to test the Calculator class. The `testAdd()` method calls the add() method and checks if it returns the expected result. The other two tests check if the subtract() and multiply() methods throw an ArithmeticException when given zero as an argument.

We then create an instance of the CalculatorTest class, call its runTest() method for each test, and print whether each test passed or not. If any tests fail, it prints an error message along with the reason why the test failed.
